calibre's own source is included here
-------------------------------------

`old_src/` is calibre's Python source, checked in unmodified -- roughly
4,800 files and 63 MB. It is the largest third-party component of this
repository by a wide margin, and it is calibre's work, not this
project's.

It is never built or executed; it is the reference the port is written
and verified against. Around 276 ported modules carry a
`Port of old_src/...` header naming the exact upstream file they derive
from, so provenance is recorded per module rather than only in this
file.

calibre's own licence text travels with it at `old_src/LICENSE`, and
is the same GPL v3 that governs this project.


RAR support is not free software
--------------------------------

This repository does not contain any UnRAR source. RAR/CBR support
comes from the `unrar` crate, which Cargo fetches at build time, and
`unrar_sys` vendors Alexander Roshal's UnRAR sources inside itself. So
nothing here needs relicensing -- but anyone building or shipping this
should know what they are linking.

The crate declares `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, which covers only the Rust
wrapper. The bundled C++ carries the UnRAR licence:

    UnRAR source code may be used in any software to handle RAR
    archives without limitations free of charge, but cannot be used to
    develop RAR (WinRAR) compatible archiver and to re-create RAR
    compression algorithm, which is proprietary.

That restricts *use*, which GPL v3 section 7 does not permit adding, so
it is GPL-incompatible and non-free by both the Debian Free Software
Guidelines and the FSF's definition.

What this does and does not affect:

  * **Source, as published here: unaffected.** No UnRAR code is in this
    repository, and everything in it is GPL v3.
  * **Building from source for your own use: unaffected.**
  * **Distributing built binaries:** the combined work cannot satisfy
    both licences, so the GPL does not authorise it, and Linux
    distributions will not package it.

To get a binary without that constraint, drop the `unrar` dependency
from `calibre_utils` and the RAR/CBR paths using it
(`calibre_utils::unrar`, `calibre_ebooks::metadata::rar`,
`input::rar_input`, and the RAR arm of `comic::input`). Everything else
is GPL-clean.


Bundled third-party material
----------------------------

Beyond the Rust dependency tree (whose licences travel with the crates
and are overwhelmingly MIT/Apache-2.0), these are checked in or shipped
inside the binary:

  * `old_src/resources/images/` -- calibre's own icon set. GPL v3, same
    as the rest of calibre.

  * `crates/calibre_srv/resources/mathjax/` -- MathJax 3.1.4,
    Apache-2.0. Vendored so mathematical content renders offline.

  * `web/public/pdfjs/` -- PDF.js runtime data (character maps and the
    standard-font set), Apache-2.0. The standard fonts additionally
    carry their own terms; `LICENSE_FOXIT` and `LICENSE_LIBERATION`
    sit beside them.

  * `crates/calibre_ebooks/resources/dictionaries/` -- Hunspell
    dictionaries. en-US is SCOWL (Kevin Atkinson, permissive); en-GB is
    LGPL. Provenance for both was established from the files rather
    than assumed -- see the NOTICE in that directory, which also
    records why calibre's own unattributed en-US is deliberately not
    used.

  * espeak-ng, reached through the `espeak-ng` crate, is GPL-3.0-or-later
    and compatible with this project's GPL v3.
